WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.220
JENNY BROWN: So far on
Cloud Storage Bytes,

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.200
we've talked about
managing object data

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.110
through renaming,
moving, and deleting.

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:09.660
But there's another
facet of managing objects

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.700
that's equally important--

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.530
managing metadata.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.570
Today we'll take a
look at what metadata

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:19.300
is available in a Cloud Storage
object and how it's used.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:20.100
Let's get started.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.341
[CHEERFUL MUSIC]

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:28.210
Metadata identifies properties
of a Cloud Storage object

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.100
and specifies how
the object should

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.500
be handled when it's accessed.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:35.540
Metadata exists as
key/value pairs.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:38.180
The mutability of
metadata varies.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:40.870
Some metadata you
can edit at any time.

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:43.600
Some metadata you can
only set at the time

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.530
the object is created, and some
metadata you can only view.

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:51.650
So, first, let's talk
about editable metadata.

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:53.830
There are two
categories of metadata

00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:55.840
that are editable by users--

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:59.350
fixed-key metadata
and custom metadata.

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:02.070
With fixed-key metadata,
the keys are set,

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.180
but you can specify a value.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:06.400
With custom metadata,
you have to specify

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.930
both a key and a value.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:12.550
You can edit this list of fixed
key metadata values for objects

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:15.250
if you have sufficient
permission to do so.

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:18.850
Cloud Storage uses identity and
access management and access

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:22.870
control lists to control access
to objects, including access

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:24.790
to the object's metadata.

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:27.190
If you'd like to know
more about access control,

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:30.760
check out our episode and
the related documentation.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:34.060
The Cache-Control metadata can
specify two different aspects

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.840
of how data is served
from Cloud Storage--

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:40.370
if it can be cached and
if it can be transformed.

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:42.490
I won't say more about
Cache-Control here

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:45.170
because that'll be enough
for a future video,

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:47.980
but you can also find out more
in the documentation linked

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:49.390
below.

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:52.120
The Content-Disposition
metadata specifies

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:56.560
presentation information about
the data being transmitted.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.990
Setting
Content-Disposition allows

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:02.300
you to control the presentation
of style of the content.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.230
For example, determining
whether an attachment should

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:07.810
be automatically
displayed or whether some

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:11.163
form of action from the user
should be required to open it.

00:02:11.163 --> 00:02:12.580
Check the links
in the description

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:15.370
to see the Content-Disposition
specification.

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:17.380
The Content-Encoding
metadata can

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:19.930
be used to indicate that
an object is compressed

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:23.140
while still maintaining the
object's underlying content

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:24.100
type.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:27.370
For example, a text file
that is gzip compressed

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:30.160
can have the fact that
it's a text file indicated

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:33.730
in the Content-Type and the
fact that it's gzip compressed

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:36.400
indicated in Content-Encoding.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.310
The Content-Language metadata
indicates the language

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:42.550
or languages that the
object is intended for.

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:47.380
Refer to ISO 639-1 language
codes for the supported values

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:48.920
of this metadata.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:52.540
The most commonly set
metadata is Content-Type, also

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:54.730
known as media type.

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:58.360
This allows browsers to
render the object properly.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:00.640
All objects of a
value is specified

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:03.190
in their Content-Type
metadata, but this value

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:06.800
does not have to match the
underlying type of the object.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.050
For example, if the
Content-Type isn't specified

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:13.480
when the object gets uploaded
and it's not a common data

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:17.410
type, chances are it's going
to be something generic or just

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:19.210
wrong in the metadata.

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:21.670
Custom metadata are
like standard metadata,

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:25.450
except you can specify custom
metadata key/value pairs.

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:30.170
To create custom metadata, you
specify both a value and a key.

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:33.370
Once you have created a custom
metadata key/value pair,

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:36.220
you can delete the key
or change the value.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:38.980
You can edit fixed-key
and custom metadata

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:43.930
from the REST API, gsutil, and
the Cloud Platform console.

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:46.300
Let's take a look
at the console.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:49.810
Open the Cloud Storage browser
in the Cloud Platform console.

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:51.790
In the list of buckets,
click on the name

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:54.610
of the bucket that
contains the desired object

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:56.960
and navigate to the object.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:59.500
Click the three dots for
more options associated

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:00.580
with the object.

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:02.410
Click Edit metadata.

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:04.270
In the overlay
window that appears,

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:06.820
make the desired
edits to the metadata.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:10.480
For standard metadata
fields, edit the value.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:12.820
Add your own custom
metadata by clicking

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:15.820
the plus sign Add Item button.

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:19.000
You can edit both the key
and the value of your custom

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:20.380
metadata.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:24.280
Delete your custom metadata
by clicking the associated X.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.190
Finally, click Save.

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:30.430
And now you know how to edit
metadata from the console.

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:33.310
But some metadata can't
be edited directly.

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:38.290
This metadata is set at the time
of object creation or rewrite.

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:40.900
As part of the object
creation or rewrite,

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:44.110
you can set some such
metadata, such as the storage

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:47.290
class of the object or
customer-supplied encryption

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:48.400
keys.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:50.590
Other metadata is
automatically added

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:53.320
and can only be viewed,
such as the generation

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:56.290
number of the object or
the time of creation.

00:04:56.290 --> 00:04:58.660
Find out more in the
docs linked below.

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:01.690
There's clearly a lot more
you can do about metadata,

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:04.280
but that's for a totally
different episode.

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:07.240
If you want to know about how
Cloud Storage uses metadata,

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.100
check out the links
in the description.

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:11.590
Don't forget to
subscribe, give us a like,

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:13.810
and tell us what you want
to learn about Google Cloud

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:14.740
Storage.

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:17.810
Thanks for joining us for this
quick byte of Cloud Storage.

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:18.880
See you next time.

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:22.230
[CHEERFUL MUSIC]

